# TypeScript rules

- The fix for a type error is NEVER `any`. Use `unknown` + narrowing, or write the real type.
- Do not use `as SomeType` to silence an error. Write a type guard. (`as const` is fine.)
- `catch (e)` is `unknown`. Narrow with `e instanceof Error` before using error properties.
- Use `satisfies` on config objects so literal types survive.
- Every union `switch` ends with `default: assertNever(x)`.

References:
- TypeScript `satisfies`: https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/release-notes/typescript-4-9.html
- TypeScript narrowing and exhaustiveness: https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/2/narrowing.html
